5-5dae-a734-40ae1f752a93", "at": 1795665538.6714952, "until": 1795665539.4141548, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 3236, "layer": "worker", "category": "execute", "kind": "tool.pending", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "498127d07fa1731dbe17c7f266bdbc29", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": "completed", "label": "execute → completed", "repository": null, "tool_call_id": "toolu_0171Xt7mycZRgKNNMnzsPwYd", "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "cd /hutian; rm -rf iw3; timeout 120 git clone -q $HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/idea-repo.wiki.git iw3 && cd iw3 && git log --format='%h %s' | head -8; git diff $(git log --format=%h | sed -n 5p) HEAD | head -150", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /hutian; rm -rf iw3; timeout 120 git clone -q $HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/idea-repo.wiki.git iw3 && cd iw3 && git log --format='%h %s' | head -8; git diff $(git log --format=%h | sed -n 5p) HEAD | head -150"]], "output": "```console\n5b58cf3 Brief rev 3: Q>=1 baseline from null, bounded f_q, leave-dataset-out, SE choice\nfdd321c typo\n3c1410e Brief rev 2: SE-unit scale, noise yardstick, identifiability, null model, priorities\n80e5e40 Brief: remove unverified author attribution\ncfeb34b Brief: idea of the study\ndiff --git a/Brief.md b/Brief.md\nindex 7a99e66..f0f776e 100644\n--- a/Brief.md\n+++ b/Brief.md\n@@ -1,45 +1,59 @@\n-# Brief: the idea\n+# Brief: the idea (revision 2, after review 1)\n \n ## Working title\n-**Significant but fragile? How much comparison data must change to change a pairwise-comparison leaderboard, and does the answer depend on the evaluation design?**\n+**Significant but fragile? How many comparisons must change to change a pairwise-comparison leaderboard, and what separates evaluation designs once statistics are accounted for?**\n \n ## The question, made operational\n-A leaderboard is a BT-type fit on N comparisons. A *conclusion* C is something a reader takes from it:\n-(C1) model *i* is above model *j* (adjacent pairs, and the top-1 vs top-2 pair); (C2) the top-1 model; (C3) the top-k set (k = 3, 5, 10); (C4) the whole order (Kendall tau against the original fit).\n-\n-For a conclusion C and a perturbation family P (delete comparisons / reverse outcomes / insert new comparisons, the last being vote rigging) we ask:\n-\n-1. **Fragility** k\\*(C, P): the smallest number of altered comparisons, found by a search, that changes C; reported as a fraction of N and of the comparisons that involve the models in C.\n-2. **Fragility against noise**: the same C under *non-adversarial* change: bootstrap resampling and random outcome-flips at rate eps. The common axis is the *adversarial advantage* A(C) = eps_50^random / (k\\*/N), i.e. how many times smaller the intentional change is than the random change that flips C half the time. This puts \"noise\" and \"intent\" on one scale instead of two literatures.\n-3. **Does the evaluation design matter, or only the statistics?** Hypothesis H2: raw k\\*/N differs by orders of magnitude across datasets, but after conditioning on statistical separation (gap in BT score / bootstrap SE of the gap) and information density (comparisons per pair and per model, comparison-graph spectral gap, tie rate) the design label adds little. The alternative H2' (design-specific structure: single-judge bias, unbalanced crowd sampling, temporal sports schedules matter beyond these covariates) is equally publishable. We decide by a pre-stated test: a model of log k\\*/N on the covariates, with and without the design factor, with cluster-bootstrap uncertainty.\n-4. **Does a confidence interval say anything about robustness?** H3: pairs whose bootstrap CIs are separated can still be reversed by a small fraction of N, so \"statistically significant\" does not mean \"robust\"; we quantify, per design, the share of CI-separated adjacent pairs with k\\*/N below set thresholds (e.g. 0.5%, 1%, 2% of N), and how well CI width / z-gap predicts k\\*.\n-\n-## Why this is new (what prior work leaves open)\n-- Arena-style reports give bootstrap CIs (sampling noise only). Work on rigging/poisoning of pairwise rankings (Min et al. 2025; Fan et al. poisoning / HodgeRank attacks) designs attacks on one system; drop-a-few-data robustness (Broderick et al. 2020, built on influence functions, Koh & Liang 2017) gives a method; neither says **whether sensitivity is a property of the evaluation design** or **how adversarial and noise scales relate**.\n-- No existing study we know of compares, on one definition of change and one set of conclusions, crowd votes, expert labels, LLM-judge labels and sports results, and *separates design from statistics*. Two controlled comparisons make this possible: (a) the **same battles** judged by crowd (Arena-55k) and by LLM judges (potsawee, same comparison graph and same model set); (b) the **same MT-Bench question/model pairs** judged by experts and by GPT-4. Differences there are due to the judge/label process only. NBA seasons (70 in\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "fb254838-0863-5315-a7c8-083a6b3b7d45", "at": 1795665539.017189, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 3238, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "498127d07fa1731dbe17c7f266bdbc29", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "95c979d1-e3c2-5611-8bbd-1d4f0ea43086", "at": 1795665539.019401, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 3239, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "498127d07fa1731dbe17c7f266bdbc29", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "3366c07c-e